# Finishing My A Levels: What I Learned Along the Way

This summer marked the end of my time at Burnham Grammar School and, with it, my A Levels.

I finished with an **A\* in Mathematics, A in Physics, A in Economics and an A\* in my EPQ**. I am proud of those results, particularly because there were plenty of points over the last two years where things did not feel nearly as certain as the final grades might make them look.

More than anything, though, finishing sixth form has made me reflect on what I actually learnt from the process.

## Consistency mattered more than intensity

One of the biggest lessons was that progress rarely came from one particularly productive evening or an unrealistic revision timetable.

It came from doing the ordinary things consistently.

Keeping up with problem sheets. Going back over mistakes. Revisiting topics I thought I understood. Asking questions when something still did not make sense.

There were definitely periods where I tried to compensate for lost time by doing too much at once. Usually, that was less effective than simply being disciplined over a longer period.

Mathematics probably reinforced this most clearly. Difficult questions became much more manageable once enough of the underlying methods had become familiar. Physics was similar: understanding the theory mattered, but so did being able to apply it carefully when the question looked unfamiliar.

That is something I want to carry into university. There is no real substitute for turning up and doing the work properly.

## Getting things wrong was useful

I also became much less afraid of getting questions wrong.

Earlier on, it was easy to look at a bad test or a difficult topic as evidence that I simply was not good enough at it. Over time, I realised that the value of a mistake depends largely on what happens afterwards.

A question I got wrong and properly understood was often more useful than one I got right without thinking very much about it.

That sounds obvious now, but it changed how I revised. Instead of avoiding the areas I was weakest at, I became more deliberate about finding them.

My EPQ taught me something similar in a different way. Researching Brexit's impact on the government's response to COVID-19 meant dealing with evidence that did not always point neatly in one direction. I had to weigh competing explanations, question the reliability of sources and accept that a strong argument is not the same thing as pretending there is no uncertainty.

It made me more comfortable changing my view when the evidence justified it.

## Asking for help is not a weakness

Another lesson was learning when to ask for help.

There is a point where trying to solve something independently is useful, and another where repeatedly staring at the same problem stops being productive.

Teachers explaining something differently, friends comparing approaches or even simply hearing someone else's question could often expose something I had completely missed.

I think there can sometimes be a temptation to make academic success look individual: one person, one set of grades, one result.

It really was not.

I was fortunate to have teachers who gave their time, friends who made difficult periods much easier, and a family that supported me throughout all of it.

I am particularly conscious of that when I think about my own family. I have grown up hearing about the work and sacrifices that allowed later generations to have opportunities that were never guaranteed. My parents have always worked hard, and that has shaped the way I look at education. A set of grades has my name next to it, but getting to the position where I could pursue them depended on far more than me.

That is something I do not want to lose sight of.

## Humility matters

A Levels also gave me a healthy reminder that there is always more to learn.

You can feel completely comfortable with a topic and then find a question which exposes a gap in your understanding within about thirty seconds.

That happened enough times to keep me grounded.

I think confidence is useful, but only when it sits alongside the willingness to admit that you do not know something. Some of the biggest improvements I made came after accepting that my first approach was not working and changing it.

That applies well beyond exams.

## Moving on

I am now starting **Economics and Finance at the University of Bristol**, which means going from being relatively comfortable with A-Level material to once again being surrounded by things I do not yet understand.

I am actually looking forward to that.

I know university will be more demanding, and I am sure there will be plenty of moments where I feel behind or get things wrong. If the last two years taught me anything, it is that this is normally part of learning rather than evidence that learning is not happening.

I am grateful for how sixth form ended, but even more grateful for the people who helped me get there.

The grades matter to me, of course. But I think the lessons I will use for longer are simpler: be consistent, ask for help, learn from mistakes, stay grounded and remember that there is always more to learn.